# Nonvanishing of the ideals (X-x, Y-y) in R[W]

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.CoordinateRing.XYIdeal_ne_bot`
(`43222cf5-702e-5313-9f7d-1a3bedce0936`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `004f37df-618f-5a67-aaad-9288f3791c2b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $R$ be a nontrivial commutative ring and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $R$ in affine form,
with affine coordinate ring $R[W] = R[X][Y]/(Y^2 + a_1XY + a_3Y - (X^3 + a_2X^2 + a_4X + a_6))$. For
any element $x \in R$ and any univariate polynomial $y \in R[X]$, the ideal `XYIdeal W x y` of
$R[W]$ — the ideal generated by the class of $X - x$ and the class of $Y - y(X)$, i.e. by `XClass W
x` and `YClass W y` — is not the zero ideal. No hypothesis is imposed relating $x$ and $y$ to the
curve: the pair need not be a point of $W$, and indeed $y$ is allowed to be an arbitrary polynomial
rather than a constant, so the conclusion is the purely algebraic assertion that these two
generators do not both vanish in $R[W]$.

For an affine point $P = (x, y)$ of $W$ this is the statement that the point ideal $\mathfrak m_P
\subset R[W]$ is nonzero; combined with its maximality it is what places $\mathfrak m_P$ in the
height-one spectrum of the coordinate ring, that is, exhibits $P$ as a finite place of the function
field of the curve. It is used throughout the development of places and divisors on genus-one curves
in this formalisation.

